Patch 29: remove the pre-trellis gate's bias instead of padding it.

Standalone against anchor 44be072 -- it contains 0028's change as well, so
apply 0029 OR 0028, never both.

THE PROBLEM WITH THE MARGIN. Patches 0027 and 0028 both compare an untrellised
estimate of a candidate's RD against best_rd, which is a trellised figure. The
estimate is biased high by however much the trellis would have lowered it, and
both patches pay for that bias with a constant margin.

The bias is not constant. How much the trellis lowers a candidate's RD depends
on qindex, on coefficient density, on transform block size and on content. CTC
showed the family sliding along one trade-off curve as that constant moves:

    margin 4/64   A1 27.7   A2 14.6
    margin 8/64   A1 42.1   A2 20.2
    marginal ratio of the step between them: 11.5 (A1), 10.8 (A2)

Every point on that curve is a different compromise between the same two
errors. 0028 picks a better point on it. This patch tries to move the curve.

THE CORRECTION IS ALREADY IN HAND. Every candidate that passes the gate has its
pre-trellis estimate computed by the gate and its true post-trellis RD computed
a few statements later. Accumulating both over the candidates of the transform
block currently being searched gives

    alpha = sum(rd_post) / sum(pre_rd)

which is the trellis's realised RD reduction, measured on exactly the
population the gate is applied to. The gate can then compare like with like:
alpha * pre_rd against best_rd.

Because de-biasing makes the estimate smaller, calibration makes the gate
SAFER at a fixed margin, not faster -- confirmed locally: at margin 8/64 the
calibrated gate is +13.77% where the uncalibrated one is +17.68%. The point of
the patch is to spend that safety on a smaller margin, so the default here is
4/64 -- the setting whose uncalibrated form measured A2 ratio 14.6 -- on the
thesis that most of that damage was bias rather than variance.

MEASURED LOCALLY (192x128, 4 frames, QP 185, cpu-used=3, three paired reps
against the same baseline binary):

    0028 margin  8/64  uncalibrated   +17.68%    (= 0027 shift 3, the CTC point)
    0028 margin 12/64  uncalibrated   +13.02%
    0029 margin  4/64  CALIBRATED     +19.07%
    0029 margin  8/64  CALIBRATED     +13.77%
    0029 margin 12/64  CALIBRATED      +9.49%

So the calibrated gate at margin 4 is somewhat faster than the uncalibrated
gate at the operating point CTC already measured. **Whether it is also better
in BD-rate is the entire question, and local timing cannot answer it.**

DETERMINISM. The accumulators are locals in search_tx_type, scoped to one
transform block's candidate loop. They are deliberately NOT carried in
MACROBLOCK: the gate's behaviour then depends only on the candidates of the
block being searched, never on tile decomposition, thread count or encode
history. Same input, same output, at any thread count.

Overflow is handled by scaling both sums down until the Q12 shift is provably
safe, rather than by assuming a bound on RD magnitudes. alpha is clamped to
(0.5, 1.0]: above 1.0 the gate would become more aggressive than its own
uncalibrated estimate, which is never intended; below the floor it would
quietly switch itself off.

    -DAVM_TX_PRE_TRELLIS_GATE_MARGIN64=N   residual margin (default 4)
    -DAVM_TX_GATE_CALIB_MIN=N              samples before alpha is trusted (2)
    -DAVM_TX_GATE_ALPHA_MIN_Q12=N          floor on alpha, 2048 == 0.5

WHAT TO RUN. Speed 3 only. One arm at the default (margin 4), and if a second
is available, margin 8. Nothing at Speed 4 -- that preset failed A2 in every
0027 arm and its absolute speedups were 1-2%.

WHAT WOULD FALSIFY IT. The thesis is that the gate's damage is dominated by
bias, so removing the bias buys more than padding it. If 0029 at margin 4 lands
on the same speed/BD line as the 0027 and 0028 points -- that is, if its ratio
is no better than what 0028 achieves at whatever margin gives the same speedup
-- then the damage is variance, not bias, and calibration is not worth its
complexity. In that case 0028 is the patch to keep and this one should be
dropped rather than tuned.

A NOTE ON WHAT THIS DOES NOT FIX. alpha is measured over the candidates of a
single transform block, so the first AVM_TX_GATE_CALIB_MIN candidates of every
block still run on the fixed margin. Widening the scope (per superblock, per
frame) would calibrate sooner but reintroduces the cross-block state that the
current design avoids for determinism. That trade is worth revisiting only if
this patch shows the mechanism works.

diff --git a/av2/encoder/tx_search.c b/av2/encoder/tx_search.c
index be3b148..29eb49c 100644
--- a/av2/encoder/tx_search.c
+++ b/av2/encoder/tx_search.c
@@ -2330,6 +2330,12 @@ static void search_tx_type(const AV2_COMP *cpi, MACROBLOCK *x, int plane,
   uint16_t best_eob = 0;
   TX_TYPE best_tx_type = DCT_DCT;
   int rate_cost = 0;
+  // Pre-trellis gate self-calibration state. Scoped to this transform block's
+  // candidate loop -- deliberately not carried in MACROBLOCK, so the gate's
+  // behaviour depends only on the candidates of the block being searched and
+  // is independent of tile decomposition and thread count.
+  int64_t gate_pre_sum = 0, gate_post_sum = 0;
+  int gate_n = 0;
   // The buffer used to swap dqcoeff in macroblockd_plane so we can keep dqcoeff
   // of the best tx_type.
   DECLARE_ALIGNED(32, tran_low_t, this_dqcoeff[MAX_SB_SQUARE]);
@@ -2626,6 +2632,146 @@ static void search_tx_type(const AV2_COMP *cpi, MACROBLOCK *x, int plane,
                                    tx_sf->prune_intra_ist_stx_by_zero_eob)) {
           continue;
         }
+        // Pre-trellis RD gate.
+        //
+        // The trellis (RDOQ, or the 8-state TCQ path) is the single most
+        // expensive step in this loop -- a callgrind profile of the encoder
+        // puts trellis quantisation at ~47% of instructions retired -- and it
+        // runs once per candidate transform type, per secondary-transform
+        // index, per transform block. Only one candidate survives.
+        //
+        // The FP-quantised coefficients for this candidate are already sitting
+        // in qcoeff after av2_quant above. Cost them, take the transform-domain
+        // distortion, and drop the candidate before the trellis runs if even
+        // that estimate cannot come near the incumbent.
+        //
+        // The estimate is PESSIMISTIC about the candidate: the trellis exists
+        // to lower rate, so the true post-trellis RD is below this one, while
+        // best_rd is already a post-trellis figure. Comparing the two directly
+        // would prune candidates that would have won. The margin pays for that
+        // bias -- the candidate is dropped only when it is worse than the
+        // incumbent by more than MARGIN64/64 of its own cost.
+        //
+        // The margin was a power-of-two shift in the first version of this
+        // gate. CTC showed that to be too coarse a control. At shift 3 (a
+        // 1/8 margin, MARGIN64 == 8):
+        //
+        //     A1  +3.79% / +0.09% BD  ratio 42.1   (bar 25, passes by 1.7x)
+        //     A2  +3.03% / +0.15% BD  ratio 20.2   (bar 25, misses by 4.8)
+        //
+        // and at shift 4 (a 1/16 margin, MARGIN64 == 4) both classes got
+        // faster and worse: A1 27.7, A2 14.6. The marginal ratio of that step
+        // is 11.5 on A1 and 10.8 on A2 -- far below the ratio each class is
+        // already operating at -- so the whole family moves the wrong way when
+        // the margin is tightened, and the correct direction is to LOOSEN it.
+        //
+        // How much? At A2's observed marginal ratio of 10.8, giving up 0.55
+        // percentage points of its 3.03% speedup lands it at
+        // (+2.48%, +0.099%) = ratio 25.0 exactly, and takes A1 to roughly
+        // (+3.11%, +0.031%) = ratio 101. That is an 18% reduction in speedup.
+        // The next power-of-two step, shift 2, doubles the margin and (on a
+        // local paired measurement) makes the gate fire so rarely that it no
+        // longer repays its own cost -- far more than an 18% give-up.
+        //
+        // So the margin is now in 64ths, and the interesting settings are
+        // between the two powers of two that CTC has already bracketed:
+        //
+        //     MARGIN64  4   drop if  6.7% worse   (= old shift 4, measured)
+        //     MARGIN64  8   drop if 14.3% worse   (= old shift 3, measured)
+        //     MARGIN64 10   drop if 18.5% worse   (NEW)
+        //     MARGIN64 12   drop if 23.1% worse   (NEW, default)
+        //     MARGIN64 16   drop if 33.3% worse   (= old shift 2)
+        //     MARGIN64  0   gate disabled entirely
+        //
+        // ("drop if X worse" is the threshold on pre_rd/best_rd, i.e.
+        //  64/(64-MARGIN64) - 1.)
+        //
+        // Note this is the same test the existing line below already performs
+        // ("if rd cost based on coeff rate alone is already more than best_rd,
+        // terminate early") -- but that one runs AFTER the trellis has been
+        // paid for, so it saves only the distortion call. This one runs before.
+        //
+        // Excluded: 64-point transforms, where three quadrants of coefficients
+        // are forced to zero and dist_block_tx_domain systematically
+        // mis-estimates; FSC blocks, which take a different optimize path; and
+        // eob == 0, which the trellis would not be called on anyway.
+        // SELF-CALIBRATION (patch 0029).
+        //
+        // The margin above is a fixed pad for a bias whose size is not fixed.
+        // How much the trellis lowers a candidate's RD depends on qindex, on
+        // coefficient density, on block size and on content -- a constant
+        // 12/64 is right for none of them, and CTC showed the whole family
+        // sliding along a trade-off curve as that constant moves rather than
+        // stepping off it.
+        //
+        // The correction is available for free. Every candidate that passes
+        // the gate has its pre-trellis estimate computed here and its true
+        // post-trellis RD computed a few statements below. Accumulating both
+        // gives alpha = sum(rd_post) / sum(pre_rd), the trellis's realised RD
+        // reduction on THIS transform block, and the gate can then compare
+        // like with like: alpha * pre_rd against best_rd.
+        //
+        // Because de-biasing makes the estimate smaller, the gate becomes
+        // SAFER at a fixed margin, not faster. The point of the patch is to
+        // spend that safety on a smaller margin: the default below is 4/64
+        // (the setting whose uncalibrated form measured A2 ratio 14.6), on the
+        // thesis that most of that damage was bias, not variance.
+        //
+        // The state is a pair of local accumulators, so it depends only on the
+        // candidates of the transform block being searched -- not on tile
+        // layout, thread count, or encode history. Same input, same output.
+#ifndef AVM_TX_PRE_TRELLIS_GATE_MARGIN64
+#define AVM_TX_PRE_TRELLIS_GATE_MARGIN64 4
+#endif
+#ifndef AVM_TX_GATE_CALIB_MIN        // samples before alpha is trusted
+#define AVM_TX_GATE_CALIB_MIN 2
+#endif
+#ifndef AVM_TX_GATE_ALPHA_MIN_Q12    // floor on alpha; 2048 == 0.5
+#define AVM_TX_GATE_ALPHA_MIN_Q12 2048
+#endif
+#if AVM_TX_PRE_TRELLIS_GATE_MARGIN64 > 0
+        int64_t this_pre_rd = -1;
+        if (quant_param.use_optimize_b && !fsc_mode_in && *eob != 0 &&
+            txw != 64 && txh != 64) {
+          const int pre_rate = cost_coeffs(cm, x, plane, block, tx_size,
+                                           packed_tx_type, CCTX_NONE, txb_ctx,
+                                           cm->features.reduced_tx_set_used);
+          int64_t pre_dist, pre_sse;
+          dist_block_tx_domain(x, plane, block, tx_size, &pre_dist, &pre_sse);
+          const int64_t pre_rd = RDCOST(x->rdmult, pre_rate, pre_dist);
+          this_pre_rd = pre_rd;
+          if (best_rd != INT64_MAX) {
+            // pre_rd is bounded well below INT64_MAX/64 here (it is an RD cost
+            // for a single transform block), so the multiply cannot overflow.
+            int64_t effective =
+                pre_rd - ((pre_rd * AVM_TX_PRE_TRELLIS_GATE_MARGIN64) >> 6);
+            if (gate_n >= AVM_TX_GATE_CALIB_MIN && gate_pre_sum > 0) {
+              // Scale both sums down until the Q12 shift is provably safe,
+              // rather than assuming a bound on RD magnitudes.
+              int64_t ps = gate_pre_sum, qs = gate_post_sum;
+              while (qs > ((int64_t)1 << 50)) {
+                qs >>= 1;
+                ps >>= 1;
+              }
+              if (ps > 0) {
+                int64_t alpha_q12 = (qs << 12) / ps;
+                // alpha > 1 would make the gate more aggressive than its own
+                // uncalibrated estimate, which is never intended; alpha below
+                // the floor would quietly switch the gate off.
+                if (alpha_q12 > 4096) alpha_q12 = 4096;
+                if (alpha_q12 < AVM_TX_GATE_ALPHA_MIN_Q12)
+                  alpha_q12 = AVM_TX_GATE_ALPHA_MIN_Q12;
+                effective = (pre_rd * alpha_q12) >> 12;
+                effective -=
+                    (effective * AVM_TX_PRE_TRELLIS_GATE_MARGIN64) >> 6;
+              }
+            }
+            if (effective > best_rd) {
+              continue;
+            }
+          }
+        }
+#endif  // AVM_TX_PRE_TRELLIS_GATE_MARGIN64 > 0
         if (fsc_mode_in && quant_param.use_optimize_b) {
           av2_optimize_fsc(cpi, x, plane, block, tx_size, packed_tx_type,
                            txb_ctx, &rate_cost);
@@ -2668,6 +2814,18 @@ static void search_tx_type(const AV2_COMP *cpi, MACROBLOCK *x, int plane,
         const int64_t rd =
             RDCOST(x->rdmult, this_rd_stats.rate, this_rd_stats.dist);
 
+#if AVM_TX_PRE_TRELLIS_GATE_MARGIN64 > 0
+        // Feed the gate's own estimate and the outcome it was estimating back
+        // into the calibration. Only candidates that were actually costed by
+        // the gate contribute, so the ratio is measured over exactly the
+        // population the gate is applied to.
+        if (this_pre_rd > 0 && rd > 0) {
+          gate_pre_sum += this_pre_rd;
+          gate_post_sum += rd;
+          gate_n++;
+        }
+#endif  // AVM_TX_PRE_TRELLIS_GATE_MARGIN64 > 0
+
         if (is_lossless) {
           assert(this_rd_stats.dist == 0);
         }
